#!/bin/sh
# post-review: posts the review a reviewer stored on its bead as a GitHub review
# on the bead's pull request, as the yard's GitHub App, and sends the bead on
# the way the review says. It is the one place the public line's reviews are
# posted from (the flow yardr.public; docs/line.md, "The public line"), and a
# pull request from outside's too (below), and the reviewers never hold what it
# holds: the App's id and key file are in the environment of the exec group that
# runs it, and in no reviewer's.
#
# Run it as the cmd of an exec group (yardr-post, limit 1), routed for the two
# posting stages of the flow: review-post, after review, and
# review-security-post, after review-security. The reviewer's part is data. In
# the one update that records the head it read (metadata reviewed_head) it
# stores its review as metadata review, a JSON object
#
#   {"outcome": "done", "body": "What the change does…",
#    "comments": [{"path": "internal/x/y.go", "line": 42, "body": "…"}]}
#
# and reports the same outcome, which takes the bead here. The outcome is what
# the review decides: done (approved, and the security review follows), done-safe
# (approved, and only the yard's tests follow) or changes (the builder is to
# change it); review-security-post takes done and changes. On the public line,
# the GitHub review is made from it: APPROVE for the two approvals and
# REQUEST_CHANGES for changes, with the verdict ("Approved", "Changes needed")
# written as the first line of the body by this script, so that the two cannot
# disagree. Each comment is on a line of the pull request's diff (the right
# side), by path in the repository and line number in the head the reviewer
# read. A question for a person is not posted: it never comes here.
#
# What it reads of the bead is its stage, its type, its tracks link, own_pr,
# reviewed_head and review, and nothing else: no body, no note. On the public
# line, the pull request is the one the bead tracks (its URL must be
# https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>/pull/<n> and nothing else), which must also
# be the bead's own_pr and be from the bead's branch of that repository: a bead
# whose metadata was written to point at another pull request is held, not
# posted on. The review is anchored to reviewed_head, and refused when GitHub
# says the pull request has another head now, so that a review is never posted
# on lines of a commit it was not written against.
#
# A bead of type pr is a pull request from outside, on the contributors pack's
# flow, whose review-post stage runs this script too (docs/line.md, "Posting a
# pull request's review"). The rules above are all kept but two, which that bead
# cannot meet: it has no own_pr, and its head is the contributor's branch, in a
# fork perhaps, not yard/<bead>. The pull request is the one its tracks link
# names, and the link must have been made by the hook that made the bead
# (created_by is hook:<name>), which a link made by a person or an agent of the
# yard is not: that one is held, not posted on. The review is always a comment
# review, never an approval or a request for changes: merging is a maintainer's,
# and a request for changes would stand on the pull request after its author
# had acted, since nothing here posts again. Its stored outcomes are done and
# changes (the pack's flow has no done-safe), the verdict is still the first
# line, and the script gives the bead no merge-ok and touches no label. What it
# sends back to the reviewer never tells it to push: the pull request is its
# author's, and the reviewer pushes nothing of it.
#
# On the public line, GitHub does not let the App approve or ask changes of the
# pull request it opened (open-pr). The review is then posted as a comment
# review instead, which is why its first line is the verdict; a yard that reviews
# under another identity gets the approval or the request as asked, with no
# change here.
#
# The token is the App's, minted for the repository by contrib/github-mint
# (the github source's mint_command, or YARDR_GITHUB_MINT in the group's cmd)
# from GITHUB_APP_ID and GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE, which the group's env_file
# supplies, exactly as merge-pr has them: the token section below is merge-pr's,
# copied. The operator's own gh login is never used. Needs gh and jq on PATH.
# The token is narrowed to pull_requests write alone (PERMS below): posting
# the review wants Pull requests write of it, and reading the pull request
# wants read (GitHub's REST docs, "Create a review for a pull request" and
# "Get a pull request").
#
# The review is the reviewer's own text, built into the request by jq from the
# fields checked here and from nothing else, never interpolated into a shell
# line. What it may say is the reviewers' stage files' rule: it describes the
# change and the code, and quotes nothing of the bead.
#
# On the public line, when the review is posted, the bead gets the label
# merge-ok if the review approves the head for the yard's tests and its merge
# (done-safe at review-post, done at review-security-post). The stored review is
# emptied so that it is posted once and a later round never finds this one, and
# the bead is reported with the outcome the review stored. That is the whole of
# the public line's yes: its reviewers do not say merge-ok. In fork mode the
# script does not change labels, so a reviewer's merge-ok for a bot's Go module
# bump stays on the bead.
#
# The outcomes at the posting stages are done, done-safe and changes, the
# review's own, and failed. failed (back to the reviewer, with the reason in the
# note) is for what the reviewer can put right: no review stored, one that is no
# JSON object or has a field wrong, an outcome the stage does not take, no head
# recorded, a pull request that has moved since the head was read, a review that
# GitHub refuses as written (a comment on a line that is not in the diff). The
# flow allows one such retry, then sends the bead to decide. Everything else
# holds the bead with a note and reports nothing, for the yard to put right, then
# unhold: no gh or jq, no tracked pull request, one that is not the bead's own or
# is not open, no App inputs, a mint that fails, and any other answer GitHub
# gives. Nothing here retries.
#
# It reads the environment the exec runner gives it: YARDR_BEAD, YARDR_BRANCH
# and YARDR_BIN. The depot's checkout is never touched: GitHub is the whole of
# it.
#
# Every outcome ends in a report or a hold: exiting without one counts as a
# failed session, and three of those hold the bead.
set -u
: "${YARDR_BEAD:?not run by an exec group}"
Y=${YARDR_BIN:-yardr}
BEAD=$YARDR_BEAD
BR=${YARDR_BRANCH:-yard/$BEAD}
echo "== $(date '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z') post-review $BEAD"

# bye takes away everything the script made, whichever of it exists. It is the
# first thing hold and report do, so that nothing is left to do once the
# session ends (the daemon stops the script as soon as it has reported), and
# the EXIT trap, for every other way out. The directory the script works in
# goes from outside it. TERM and INT are ignored while it runs, so that a stop
# of the session that comes meanwhile cannot leave it half done.
bye() {
  trap '' TERM INT
  cd /
  [ -z "${work:-}" ] || rm -rf "${work:?}"
  return 0
}
trap 'bye' EXIT
# A signal that kills the script does not run its EXIT trap in every shell
# (dash's does not), so TERM and INT exit, which does, as a shell would, 128+n.
trap 'exit 143' TERM
trap 'exit 130' INT

# Reporting ends this session (the daemon stops the script), so it is
# always the last thing done, and bye the first. after is what a hold says once
# the review is on the pull request: the report is all that is left to do, and
# posting it again would put it there twice.
after=
hold() { bye; echo "== hold $BEAD: $1"; "$Y" bead note "$BEAD" "post-review: $1"; "$Y" bead hold "$BEAD"; exit 0; }
merge_ok_added=0
report() {
  bye
  echo "== $BEAD $1"
  message=$2
  [ "$merge_ok_added" != 1 ] || message="$message; merge-ok is on $BEAD"
  printf '%s\n' "post-review: $message" | "$Y" bead "done" "$BEAD" --outcome "$1" --note - && exit 0
  if [ "$STAGE" = review-post ] && [ "$1" = done-safe ] && [ "$merge_ok_added" = 1 ]; then
    if "$Y" bead update "$BEAD" --remove-label merge-ok >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      after=" The review is already posted on the pull request, but merge-ok was removed because the yard refused done-safe. Do not unhold it, which would post the review again; send it to the security review by hand: yardr bead advance $BEAD --to review-security."
    else
      after=" The review is already posted on the pull request, but merge-ok could not be removed. Do not unhold it; remove merge-ok, then send it to the security review: yardr bead update $BEAD --remove-label merge-ok && yardr bead advance $BEAD --to review-security."
    fi
  fi
  hold "could not report $1 (does the bead's flow name it for its stage? a done-safe is refused for a diff that touches .yardr/security_paths): $2$after"
}
work=$(mktemp -d) && errf=$work/err
# gh is run from a directory that is no checkout, so nothing it does to a
# local branch can reach anyone's work.
cd "$work" || exit 1

command -v gh >/dev/null 2>&1 || hold "gh is not on PATH; not posting."
command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1 || hold "jq is not on PATH; not posting."

# With --json yardr reports an error as {"error": ...} on stdout. Capture
# stderr for the hold note, then replay it so warnings stay in the group's log.
bead=$("$Y" bead show "$BEAD" --json 2>"$errf") || {
  reason=$({ cat "$errf"; printf '%s' "$bead"; } | head -c 500)
  cat "$errf" >&2
  hold "reading $BEAD failed: $reason"
}
cat "$errf" >&2
stage=$(printf '%s' "$bead" | jq -r '.stage // empty')
# A bead of type pr is a pull request from outside, which has the fork rules
# below; every other bead is the public line's own.
fork=
[ "$(printf '%s' "$bead" | jq -r '.type // empty')" != pr ] || fork=1
posting="review-post or review-security-post"
[ -z "$fork" ] || posting="review-post"
NOT="not posting" STAGE=$stage
# The outcomes of the stored review each posting stage takes, as a failed
# report names them; the table that holds them for the check is below.
yes=
case $stage in
review-post) outcomes="done, done-safe or changes" ;;
review-security-post) outcomes="done or changes" ;;
*) hold "$BEAD is in ${stage:-no stage}, and a review is posted from $posting; $NOT." ;;
esac
# The contributors pack's flow has the one posting stage, and no done-safe.
if [ -n "$fork" ]; then
  [ "$stage" = review-post ] ||
    hold "$BEAD is in $stage, and a review is posted from $posting; $NOT."
  outcomes="done or changes"
fi

# The pull request is the one the bead tracks, by the URL of the link and
# nothing else: owner, repository and number are read from it, and a URL of
# another shape (another host, an issue, a path past the number) is refused. It
# must be the one open-pr opened for the bead, which records it as own_pr; for a
# pull request from outside, which has no own_pr, the one the hook linked.
url=$(printf '%s' "$bead" | jq -r '[.links[]? | select(.kind == "tracks" and .source == "github") | .url] | first // empty')
[ -n "$url" ] ||
  hold "$BEAD tracks no GitHub pull request (no tracks link of source github); $NOT. Link it with yardr link, or take the bead out of $STAGE."
printf '%s' "$url" | grep -Eq '^https://github\.com/[A-Za-z0-9]([A-Za-z0-9-]*[A-Za-z0-9])?/[A-Za-z0-9._-]+/pull/[1-9][0-9]*$' ||
  hold "$BEAD tracks $url, which is not a pull request address (https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>/pull/<n>); $NOT."
if [ -n "$fork" ]; then
  # The link is the one thing that says which pull request the bead is of, and
  # nothing of the bead's metadata can: the branch is the contributor's. The
  # hook makes it with the bead (created_by hook:<name>). A link an agent makes
  # from its pane is recorded as the agent's, whatever actor the command names,
  # and an agent may not change the tracks link of a pr bead past triage at all;
  # this check does not rest on either.
  by=$(printf '%s' "$bead" | jq -r --arg url "$url" '[.links[]? | select(.kind == "tracks" and .source == "github" and .url == $url) | .created_by // ""] | first // empty')
  case $by in
  hook:?*) ;;
  *) hold "$BEAD's tracks link to $url was not made by a hook (made by ${by:-no one on record}), and a review is posted only on the pull request the hook linked when it made the bead; $NOT." ;;
  esac
else
  [ "$(printf '%s' "$bead" | jq -r '.metadata.own_pr // empty')" = "$url" ] ||
    hold "$BEAD's tracked pull request was not opened for its own branch by open-pr (its own_pr is not $url); $NOT."
fi
path=${url#https://github.com/}
owner=${path%%/*}
rest=${path#*/}
repo=${rest%%/*}
case $repo in . | ..) hold "$BEAD tracks $url, which is not a pull request address (https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>/pull/<n>); $NOT." ;; esac
slug=$owner/$repo

# The stored review, checked field by field; the request is made from what
# passes. 65536 is GitHub's limit on a body, and a review of more than 50
# comments is not a review a person reads. What is wrong with it is the
# reviewer's to put right, so each of these sends the bead back to it.
fix="Store the review again (yardr bead update $BEAD --meta review=<JSON>), then report the outcome it names."
printf '%s' "$bead" | jq -c '.metadata.review // ""' >"$work/stored"
[ "$(cat "$work/stored")" != '""' ] ||
  report failed "$BEAD has no stored review (metadata review), so there is nothing to post. $fix"
jq -e 'type == "object"' "$work/stored" >/dev/null 2>&1 ||
  report failed "the review stored on $BEAD is no JSON object (outcome, body, comments); text that does not parse as JSON is stored as the text it is. $fix"
review=$(jq -c '
  def text(n): type == "string" and length > 0 and (utf8bytelength <= n);
  if (.outcome | IN("done", "done-safe", "changes")) | not then error("outcome is done, done-safe or changes")
  elif (.body | text(60000)) | not then error("body is the review text, 1 to 60000 bytes")
  elif ((.comments // []) | type != "array" or length > 50) then error("comments is a list of at most 50")
  elif ((.comments // []) | all(
        (.path | type == "string" and length > 0 and length <= 500 and (startswith("/") | not) and (split("/") | all(. != ".." and . != "." and . != "")))
        and (.line | type == "number" and . >= 1 and . == floor)
        and (.body | text(10000)))) | not
    then error("each comment is {path, line, body}: a path in the repository, a line of the diff from 1, and its text")
  else {outcome, body, comments: [(.comments // [])[] | {path, line, side: "RIGHT", body}]}
  end' "$work/stored" 2>"$errf") ||
  report failed "the review stored on $BEAD is not postable: $(sed -n 's/^jq: error (at [^)]*): //p' "$errf" | head -n 1). $fix"
outcome=$(printf '%s' "$review" | jq -r .outcome)
# done-safe sends the bead to the yard's tests alone, with merge-ok, and a pull
# request from outside has no such way: its flow would refuse the report after
# the review was posted.
[ -z "$fork" ] || [ "$outcome" != done-safe ] ||
  report failed "the review stored on $BEAD says $outcome, which $STAGE does not take for a pull request from outside (it takes $outcomes). $fix"
case $stage:$outcome in
review-post:done | review-post:done-safe | review-post:changes | review-security-post:done | review-security-post:changes) ;;
*) report failed "the review stored on $BEAD says $outcome, which $STAGE does not take (it takes $outcomes). $fix" ;;
esac
case $outcome in
changes) event=REQUEST_CHANGES verdict="Changes needed" ;;
*) event=APPROVE verdict=Approved ;;
esac
# A pull request from outside gets a comment review whatever the outcome: it is
# not the yard's to approve, and a request for changes would stay on it after its
# author acts, since nothing posts again. The verdict is still the first line.
[ -z "$fork" ] || event=COMMENT
case $stage:$outcome in review-post:done-safe | review-security-post:done) yes=1 ;; esac

lower() { printf '%s' "$1" | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]'; }
short() { printf '%s' "$1" | cut -c1-7; }
# The review is of the head the reviewer recorded as read, and of that head
# alone: a full sha, which is also what the request is anchored to.
read_head=$(printf '%s' "$bead" | jq -r '.metadata.reviewed_head // empty')
case $read_head in *[!0-9a-fA-F]*) read_head= ;; esac
case ${#read_head} in 40 | 64) ;; *) read_head= ;; esac
# The reviewer of a pull request from outside has pushed nothing, and is not told
# to: its worktree stands on the head it read.
if [ -z "$read_head" ] && [ -n "$fork" ]; then
  report failed "$BEAD has no reviewed_head, the head the review is of (a full commit sha). Record the head you read and store the review in one update: yardr bead update $BEAD --meta reviewed_head=\"\$(git rev-parse HEAD)\" --meta review=<JSON>."
fi
[ -n "$read_head" ] ||
  report failed "$BEAD has no reviewed_head, the head the review is of (a full commit sha). Push your commits (git push origin $BR), then record it and store the review in one update: yardr bead update $BEAD --meta reviewed_head=\"\$(git rev-parse HEAD)\" --meta review=<JSON>."

# The token's permissions, the least the gh calls below need: posting the
# review wants Pull requests write of the token, and reading the pull request
# wants read (GitHub's REST docs, "Create a review for a pull request" and
# "Get a pull request"). The token section is merge-pr's, copied (a test
# holds them equal), with this set above it, as merge-pr has its own.
PERMS='{"pull_requests":"write"}'
# --- github_binding: the token, merge-pr's text from "The token" to its end ---
# The token: minted for this repository alone, from the App's id and key file
# the group's env_file gives, and held in this process's memory for the calls
# below. It is passed to gh in its environment, never in an argument, and is
# printed nowhere. The mint command is the github source's own unless the
# group's cmd names another. The token is narrowed to the permissions PERMS
# names, each script's own least set above, which the mint posts in the
# access_tokens body; a name or level outside the mint's list refuses the mint.
[ -n "${GITHUB_APP_ID:-}" ] && [ -n "${GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE:-}" ] ||
  hold "this group's environment has no GITHUB_APP_ID and GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE, which the yard's GitHub App is minted from (the group's env_file); $NOT."
mint=${YARDR_GITHUB_MINT:-}
[ -n "$mint" ] || mint=$("$Y" source show github --json 2>/dev/null | jq -r '.source.mint_command // empty')
[ -n "$mint" ] ||
  hold "the github source has no mint_command and the group's cmd sets no YARDR_GITHUB_MINT; $NOT. Install contrib/github-mint, then yardr source set github mint_command=<its path>."
# mint_command has the same space-split, no-shell contract as yardr's source
# mint. Disable pathname expansion while forming argv so a configured `*`
# stays an argument and never grows into files in the runner's directory.
mint_token() {
  set -f
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  set -- $mint
  set +f
  [ "$#" -gt 0 ] || return 1
  YARDR_SOURCE_BASE=https://api.github.com "$@" 2>/dev/null
}
tokjson=$(jq -nc --arg id "$GITHUB_APP_ID" --arg key "$GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE" --arg scope "$slug" --argjson permissions "$PERMS" \
  '{inputs: {GITHUB_APP_ID: $id, GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE: $key}, scope: $scope, permissions: $permissions}' |
  mint_token) ||
  hold "minting a token for $slug failed; the mint's output is not copied to the bead. $NOT."
token=$(printf '%s' "$tokjson" | jq -r '.token // empty')
unset tokjson
[ -n "$token" ] || hold "minting a token for $slug answered no token; $NOT."
gh_() { GH_TOKEN=$token gh "$@"; }
# --- end of github_binding ---

pr=$(gh_ pr view "$url" --json state,headRefOid,headRefName,headRepository,headRepositoryOwner 2>"$errf") ||
  hold "reading pull request $url failed: $(head -c 500 "$errf")"
[ "$(printf '%s' "$pr" | jq -r '.state // empty')" = OPEN ] || hold "pull request $url is not open; $NOT."
# The pull request must be the bead's own branch of this repository. own_pr is
# metadata, which an agent can write, so it is the pull request that says whose
# it is: a bead pointed at some other pull request of the App's installation is
# not posted on. A pull request from outside is the contributor's branch, and
# was bound to its bead by the link above.
if [ -z "$fork" ]; then
  headrepo=$(printf '%s' "$pr" | jq -r 'if .headRepositoryOwner.login and .headRepository.name then .headRepositoryOwner.login + "/" + .headRepository.name else empty end')
  { [ "$(printf '%s' "$pr" | jq -r '.headRefName // empty')" = "$BR" ] && [ "$(lower "$headrepo")" = "$(lower "$slug")" ]; } ||
    hold "pull request $url is not from $BR of $slug, the branch that open-pr publishes for $BEAD, so it is not $BEAD's to review; $NOT."
fi
live=$(printf '%s' "$pr" | jq -r '.headRefOid // empty')
if [ -n "$fork" ] && [ "$(lower "$live")" != "$(lower "$read_head")" ]; then
  report failed "$url has moved since the review was written (you read $(short "$read_head"), its head is $(short "$live")), and a review is posted only on the head it was written against. Read the pull request at the head it has now (your worktree stands on it: git rev-parse HEAD), then record that head and store the review of it in one update: yardr bead update $BEAD --meta reviewed_head=\"\$(git rev-parse HEAD)\" --meta review=<JSON>."
fi
[ "$(lower "$live")" = "$(lower "$read_head")" ] ||
  report failed "$url has moved since the review was written (you read $(short "$read_head"), its head is $(short "$live")), and a review is posted only on the head it was written against. If you made commits that are not pushed, push them (git push origin $BR). Read the pull request as it is now, then record that head and store the review of it in one update: yardr bead update $BEAD --meta reviewed_head=\"\$(git rev-parse HEAD)\" --meta review=<JSON>."

num=${url##*/}
request=$(printf '%s' "$review" | jq -c --arg event "$event" --arg verdict "$verdict" --arg head "$read_head" \
  '{event: $event, body: ($verdict + "\n\n" + .body), comments, commit_id: $head}')
post() {
  printf '%s' "$1" | gh_ api --method POST "repos/$slug/pulls/$num/reviews" --input - 2>&1
}
# GitHub's 422 is its answer to a review it cannot take as written, which the
# reviewer can put right; any other refusal is the yard's.
refused() {
  case $1 in
  *"HTTP 422"*) report failed "GitHub refused the review as stored: $(printf '%s' "$1" | head -c 500). A comment must be on a line of the pull request's diff, and a finding that is not on one goes in the body. $fix" ;;
  *) hold "GitHub refused the review: $(printf '%s' "$1" | head -c 500). Not posted; $NOT." ;;
  esac
}
if out=$(post "$request"); then
  :
elif [ "$event" != COMMENT ] && printf '%s' "$out" | grep -qi 'own pull request'; then
  # The account that opened the pull request cannot approve it or ask changes
  # of it; the review is the same, as a comment review.
  event=COMMENT
  out=$(post "$(printf '%s' "$request" | jq -c '.event = "COMMENT"')") || refused "$out"
else
  refused "$out"
fi
where=$(printf '%s' "$out" | jq -r '.html_url // empty' 2>/dev/null)

# The review is on the pull request from here on, and a rerun would put it
# there twice, so a hold from here says to move the bead by hand and not to
# unhold it. Recorded in one write: the yes where the review is it, and the
# review taken off the bead, so that it is not posted twice and no later round
# finds it.
set -- --meta 'review=""'
[ -z "$yes" ] || set -- "$@" --add-label merge-ok
case $stage:$outcome in
review-post:done | review-post:done-safe) move="yardr bead advance $BEAD --to review-security" ;;
review-post:changes | review-security-post:changes) move="yardr bead advance $BEAD --to new" ;;
review-security-post:done) move="yardr bead advance $BEAD --to ciwait" ;;
*) move="yardr bead advance $BEAD --to <the stage $outcome leads to>" ;;
esac
# The contributors pack's flow leads done to the yard's tests, and changes to the
# pull request's author.
if [ -n "$fork" ]; then
  case $outcome in
  done) move="yardr bead advance $BEAD --to ciwait" ;;
  *) move="yardr bead advance $BEAD --to contributor" ;;
  esac
fi
"$Y" bead update "$BEAD" "$@" >"$errf" 2>&1 ||
  hold "posted the $event review${where:+ ($where)} on $url, but recording that on $BEAD failed: $(head -c 500 "$errf"). Do not unhold it, which would post the review again: record it by hand (yardr bead update $BEAD $*) and move the bead on ($move)."
[ -z "$yes" ] || merge_ok_added=1
after=" The $event review is on the pull request${where:+ ($where)} and the stored review is empty. Do not unhold it, which would post the review again: move the bead on by hand ($move)."

report "$outcome" "posted a $event review ($outcome) with $(printf '%s' "$review" | jq '.comments | length') inline comments on $url${where:+: $where}."
